sections: INPUT, OUTPUT, COMMENTS, STAMPS①
{

  !!!
  FOOP-86 §6.2 route 3 -- RECOORDINATION.

  A statement whose settled value is a brane brings that brane's members into
  this context. When one of them is a null-characterized name ALREADY defined
  here with a DIFFERENT value, the brane cannot be coordinated in.

  Route 3 is the one route that does NOT halt its brane. The brane SEGREGATES
  the run-time error: only the statement holding the value settles NK, and it
  is an ORDINARY NK -- there is no unsteppable-versus-steppable NK. The
  receiving brane did its part, so its other statements step normally (§6.4c).

  Rendering follows §6.5's ordinary NK-reverts-to-Foolish rule, NOT §5.2's
  brane exception. That exception exists for a ROLLUP NK -- a brane NK on
  account of something inside it -- whose resolved members are worth showing.
  Here the coordination NEVER HAPPENED, so printing the would-be-coordinated
  brane would show a result that does not exist.
  !!!

  A = {'C = 1};

  !! 'C is already 2 here, so coordinating A in would give 'C a second
  !! meaning. THIS statement is the unsteppable one: it reverts to its
  !! written `A` and carries an NK annotation. Note what does NOT happen --
  !! B is not halted, 'C = 2 is untouched, and `after` below still steps.
  B = {'C = 2; D = A; after = 7};

  !! A itself is a perfectly good brane. The conflict is with the RECEIVING
  !! context, not with A, so every access into A resolves normally.
  a_is_fine = A.'C;

  !! The SAME value is not a conflict -- coordinating it in is permitted.
  ok = {'C = 1; E = A};
}
①
{
  A = {
    'C = 1
  };
  B = {
    'C = 2;
    D = A;  !! NK: 'C already defined in context
    after = 7
  };
  aˍisˍfine = 1;
  ok = {
    'C = 1;
    E = {
      'C = 1
    }
  }
}①
